cartridge "LAST NINJA (AHOY! 1986)/C.M.BLAKEMORE/1986"

' LAST NINJA (AHOY! 1986)
' A ColecoVision game based on "The Last Ninja" by Cleveland M. Blakemore,
' Ahoy! September 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, loaded and
' started by the two-line LAST NINJA BOOT). Ahoy!'s own type-in, not System
' 3's commercial game of the same name: nothing of that game is used here.
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (watched in jsc64,
' numbers read from its machine code, orig/ninja.dis); the game is drawn
' for the ColecoVision: a moonlit castle gallery that scrolls as the hero
' advances, a masked hero in light blue (two-colour sprite), rival fighters
' with staffs and fantasy dragons, chi gauge, sound effects, a title tune.
'
' The game: walk right through the gallery. Missiles fly in from the right
' (a wisp and a spinning star); open pits in the floor must be leapt. Every
' 2048 pixels of progress a foe comes: ten foes, each tougher than the
' last (fighters, then dragons, then the master), each needing one more
' hit than the one before. Hits, missiles and pits drain your chi; beating
' a foe gives some back. Beat all ten, or lose your chi, and the game tells
' you the rank you reached: ADEPT, SHUGYO, YUDANSHA, TIGER, DRAGON, NINJA,
' SHINOBI, SENSEI, SHIDOSHI, JONIN (the original's ranks).
'
' From the original (orig/): one pass of its main loop per frame does it
' all: the wisp moves 3 pixels a pass and the star 2, each coming back at a
' random height when it leaves; the hero walks 1 pixel a pass between X 16
' and 112 (C64 $19-$82) and pushes the gallery along at the right; up+right
' is a leap that scrolls a pixel every pass, rising 10 pixels at passes 20,
' 45 and 70 and landing at 95; up+left jumps in place (rising at 20, 40,
' 60, landing at 80); down crouches; up shows a star and the button throws
' it (6 pixels a pass; the hero waits until it is gone); the button strikes
' with the sword: no harm comes while striking, and a strike or a star
' takes one hit off a foe. A foe has as many hit points as its number
' (0-9; one more hit beats it), walks 1 pixel every 7,7,6,6,5,5,4,4,3,3
' passes and throws a missile at speed number/2 + 1; touching it pushes it
' back 10 pixels, and the hero too (unless striking). Chi 38 (the C64's
' gauge from X 195 down to 157): a missile or a touch costs 1, a pit 10,
' a beaten foe gives back its number. A pit every 512 pixels, 48 wide; the
' C64's gallery shifted a column every 8 pixels and a new foe came every
' 256 columns. The rank names and their order.
'
' Liberties: the gallery scrolls a whole column (8 pixels) at a time (the
' C64 scrolled smoothly); the C64 ran its loop about twice a frame, so the
' port, at one pass a frame, plays at about half the C64's speed (a human-
' paced bot finds that fair); the button alone strikes (the C64 needed
' fire+right) and a strike lasts 12 frames, then the button must be let go
' and 16 frames pass before the next (the C64 protected the hero for as
' long as the button was held, which made the foes harmless); the star
' flies straight (the C64's slope came from the walking counter); a star
' that meets a foe's missile stops there and the missile forms again at
' the foe (the C64's missile flew on unseen), and a foe within 40
' pixels of the hero throws no new missile (on the C64 a missile re-formed
' at a close foe's side, already touching the hero); a pit the hero falls into
' closes behind him (the C64 moved it away) and he stands up at X 76; a
' hit in the air does not knock him back (on the C64 it could drop him
' into the pit he was leaping); chi
' stops at 38; collisions are boxes on the drawn shapes (the C64 used its
' sprite collision register, which missed two things at once); a foe that
' gets past the hero simply leaves, as on the C64; beating the tenth foe
' ends the game at JONIN with a message (the C64 went back to its title);
' a beaten foe gives back its number + 5 chi (the C64: its number; at
' half speed even a near-perfect player rarely got past foe 4 that way:
' sim.mjs, 4 wins in 20; with +5 a 12-frame-late player wins 12 in 20).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2). Stick right/left walk, up+right leap,
' up+left jump, down crouch, up + button throw a star, button strike. On
' the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

